Comparable in Java:

Comparable is an interface in Java used to define the natural ordering of objects.

Suppose you create a Student class. Java doesn't know whether students should be sorted by:

id
name
marks
age

So, you tell Java the default sorting rule by implementing Comparable.

Why Comparable?

Primitive types already know how to compare.

10 < 20
'A' < 'B'

But for objects,

Student s1 = new Student(101, "Ram");
Student s2 = new Student(102, "Shyam");

Java doesn't know

Is s1 > s2 ?
Is s1 < s2 ?

because Student is our own class.

Comparable teaches Java how to compare two Student objects.

Definition
public interface Comparable<T>

Method

int compareTo(T obj);

You must override

compareTo()
Syntax
class Student implements Comparable<Student> {

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student other) {
        // comparison logic
    }
}
Return Values

Very important.

compareTo() returns

Negative  -> Current object is smaller

Zero      -> Both are equal

Positive  -> Current object is greater

Example

this.age = 20
other.age = 25

20 - 25 = -5

Negative

Current object comes first.

